tion. \"Candidate C sometimes not good at taking criticism\" indicates adaptability/feedback orientation. \"Candidate B can be grumpy\" and \"uncooperative\" maybe feedback orientation. \"Candidate D is regarded as arrogant\" maybe feedback orientation. \"Candidate A difficulty communicating ideas\" maybe feedback. \"Candidate A is egocentric\" maybe feedback. \"Candidate A not willing to further his education\" maybe adaptability.\n\nWe need to provide observations for each candidate regarding attention to detail, information accuracy, and adaptability/feedback orientation.\n\nWe must only use explicit facts. So for each candidate, list observations:\n\nCandidate A: 100% reliable (reliability, maybe attention), but difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, not willing to further education. No explicit attention fact. So we can say we have no explicit observation on attention to detail or information accuracy, but we have reliability. For adaptability/feedback orientation: not willing to further education suggests low adaptability, but that's inference. Must not infer. So we can only say we have no explicit info on adaptability/feedback orientation for A.\n\nCandidate B: very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather conditions, excellent computer skills, can be grumpy, uncooperative. No explicit attention. No explicit adaptability. So we can say no explicit observations.\n\nCandidate C: can anticipate dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership, sometimes not good at taking criticism, can be unorganized. So attention: anticipating dangerous situations and seeing complex connections suggests good attention to detail. Information accuracy: maybe spatial vision and complex connections. Adaptability/feedback: sometimes not good at taking criticism indicates poor feedback orientation.\n\nCandidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well